.TH GOLEM-EXEC 1 2026-11-03 golem-team-shield "User Commands"
.SH NAME
golem-exec \- run every shell command an AI coding agent starts inside a kernel jail
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B golem-exec install
.RB [ \-\-profile
.IR profile ]
.br
.B golem-exec install \-\-require
.br
.B golem-exec install \-\-refresh
.br
.B golem-exec status
.br
.B golem-exec test
.br
.B golem-exec uninstall
.RB [ \-\-purge ]
.br
.B golem-exec version
.br
.B golem-exec help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B golem-exec
is a shell shim.
Claude Code runs it as its shell, and it runs the real shell, with the same
arguments, inside a kernel jail: Seatbelt on macOS, Landlock and seccomp on
Linux, a restricted token on Windows.
Everything the command starts inherits the jail.
.PP
Codex, Gemini CLI, Cursor and Copilot CLI have no setting that names their
shell, so on macOS and Linux
.B golem-exec
is their pre-tool hook instead
.RB ( "golem-exec hook" " " \fIagent\fR ),
and rewrites each shell command to
.IB ~/.golem/bin/hook/bash " \-c '" command '
(or
.BR zsh ),
which the agent then asks about and runs as usual.
Cursor and Copilot CLI refuse the command if the hook fails; Codex and
Gemini CLI run it unjailed.
.PP
Run by its own name it manages that setup.
Run under a shell's name
.RB ( golem\-zsh ,
.BR golem\-bash ,
or
.B bash.exe
on Windows) it is the shim.
.PP
With the default settings a jailed command cannot read private SSH and GPG
keys,
.IR .netrc ,
.I .git\-credentials
or shell history; cannot read or write browser profiles, chat apps,
password managers' data, mail or Messages; cannot write to
.IR ~/.ssh ,
shell startup files, git config,
.I ~/Library/LaunchAgents
or the agents' settings and hooks; and loses secret-shaped environment
variables.
It keeps the network, cloud credentials, the SSH agent and the Docker socket.
.PP
It jails shell commands only: an agent's own file and fetch tools do not run
through a shell.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install
Copy this binary into
.I ~/.golem/bin
under the shell names, write
.I ~/.golem/shield.json
if it is missing, point Claude Code at the shim through
.B CLAUDE_CODE_SHELL
in
.I ~/.claude/settings.json
(on Windows,
.B CLAUDE_CODE_GIT_BASH_PATH
and the PowerShell tool turned off), add its hook to Codex
.RI ( ~/.codex/hooks.json ),
Gemini CLI
.RI ( ~/.gemini/settings.json )
and Cursor
.RI ( ~/.cursor/hooks.json )
where each is installed (on Windows only with
.BR \-\-all\-agents ,
and never Cursor's), and run the self-test.
Codex asks once to approve the new hook.
A shell you set in Claude Code yourself is left alone.
It refuses where the jail cannot be built, and does nothing where the Golem
agent manages the shield.
Restart your agents afterwards.
.TP
.BI "install \-\-profile " profile
As
.BR install ,
starting from
.I profile
.RB ( standard ", " strict " or " allowlist ;
see
.BR PROFILES )
instead of
.BR standard .
On a machine that already has
.IR ~/.golem/shield.json ,
it sets
.B profile
there and takes out the settings the profile decides, keeping any set tighter
than the profile would set it.
.B \-\-strict
is
.BR "\-\-profile strict" .
.TP
.B install \-\-all\-agents
As
.BR install ,
and also jail the agents whose own approval would see only the jail's
wrapper: Codex, Gemini CLI and Copilot CLI on Windows, and Devin
.RI ( ~/.config/devin/config.json ).
It sets
.B jail_past_approval
in
.I ~/.golem/shield.json
and adds their hooks. One "always allow" in those agents could then approve
every later command, each still jailed.
.TP
.B firewall install
On Windows, as administrator: install the Windows Filtering Platform filters
that refuse an allowed-hosts command every connection but the one to its proxy.
They touch only commands the shim marks, and persist until
.BR "firewall remove" .
.TP
.B apparmor install
On Ubuntu, as root: let the jail make the network namespace an allowed-hosts
command is held by.
Ubuntu allows user namespaces only to programs AppArmor names, so this puts a
copy of golem-exec in
.I /usr/local/libexec/golem-exec
and a profile for it in
.IR /etc/apparmor.d/golem-exec .
The copy only sets up each command's namespace and hands it to your own
golem-exec, and the command holds no capability.
Later versions keep using it;
.B status
says if one ever needs it installed again, and until then commands fall back
to Landlock's port rule.
.B apparmor remove
takes both out again.
.TP
.B install \-\-require
As
.BR install ,
and set
.B require_jail
in
.IR ~/.golem/shield.json :
every agent here must be jailed (see
.BR "REQUIRING THE JAIL" ).
.TP
.B install \-\-refresh
Replace an installed shim with this binary and change nothing else, unless
the jail is required here: then it also puts back each agent's shell setting or
hook that was taken out.
It does nothing when no shim is installed or the Golem agent manages the
shield.
Package managers run it after an upgrade, because your agents run the copy in
.IR ~/.golem/bin ,
not the one on your PATH.
.TP
.B status
Show the installed shim, Claude Code's shell setting, whether each agent's
commands are jailed, whether the jail can be built here, the settings file, its
profile and any setting that outranks it, and the policy commands get.
.TP
.B test
Run a plain command through the shim, which must succeed, and a read of
.IR ~/.golem ,
which the jail must deny.
.TP
.B uninstall
Put Claude Code's shell back, take the hooks out of the other agents, and
remove the shim.
.B \-\-purge
also removes the settings and the cached policies.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH SETTINGS
.I ~/.golem/shield.json
holds the policy every command gets when no Golem agent answers.
Changes apply to the next command.
.TP
.B profile
A named starting point (see
.BR PROFILES ).
A new install writes
.BR standard .
.TP
.B enforce
.B false
runs commands without a jail.
.TP
.B lockdown
Every command loses the network, the SSH agent, Docker, cloud credentials and
writes outside the project it runs in.
On Windows, which cannot take the network from a command, commands do not run
at all.
.TP
.BR ssh_agent ", " docker ", " cloud_credentials
The three grants: the SSH agent and keychain, the Docker socket, and the cloud
and package-registry credential files and variables.
.TP
.BR env_allow ", " env_deny
Environment variables to keep or remove, each a name or a prefix ending in
.BR * .
Lockdown ignores
.BR env_allow .
.TP
.B writes
.B \(dqworkspace\(dq
keeps writes to the project (the git repository a command runs in, or its
working directory outside one), temp, the tool caches
.RI ( ~/.cache ", " ~/.npm ", " ~/go/pkg/mod ", " ~/Library/Caches
and others) and
.BR allow_write ;
.B \(dqanywhere\(dq
denies only the protected files.
On Windows the shell runs at low integrity and the writable directories are
labelled low, a label they keep.
.TP
.B allow_write
More places a
.B \(dqworkspace\(dq
command may write: absolute paths, or paths under
.IR ~/ .
.TP
.B network
.B \(dqallowlist\(dq
lets commands reach only
.BR allowed_hosts ,
through a proxy
.B golem\-exec
runs for each command; every other connection, DNS included, is refused, and a
refused host is named on the command's standard error.
.B \(dqopen\(dq
leaves the network alone.
On Linux a network namespace holds it, or where the kernel allows none,
Landlock's port rule, which lets a program that ignores the proxy reach any host
on the proxy's port.
On Ubuntu the namespace needs
.BR "apparmor install" .
On Windows it is held by the filters
.B firewall install
puts in place; without them only programs that use the proxy are held to the
list.
.TP
.B allowed_hosts
Each
.IR example.com ,
.I *.example.com
(its subdomains),
.B *
(any public host), or a preset that stands for a set of hosts:
.B @model\-apis
(the major model APIs),
.B @package\-registries
(npm, PyPI, the Go module proxy, crates.io, Maven Central and RubyGems) or
.B @git\-hosts
(GitHub and GitLab).
The jail expands a preset each time a command runs;
.B status
names each and how many hosts it opens.
.B localhost
opens loopback, a test server the command starts included; without it loopback
is refused.
Link-local addresses are never reached.
With the network confined, macOS also refuses
.BR open ,
.B osascript
and
.BR shortcuts ,
which would hand a URL to another app.
.PP
A jailed command can neither read nor write
.IR ~/.golem ,
so the agent being watched cannot change its own jail.
.TP
.B require_jail
.B true
says every agent here must be jailed (see
.BR "REQUIRING THE JAIL" ).
.SH REQUIRING THE JAIL
With
.B require_jail
set, a command the jail cannot hold (Linux without Landlock) is refused with
exit status 126 rather than run without it;
.B enforce
.B false
no longer turns the jail off;
.B status
and
.BR golem\-check (1)
report an agent whose commands run outside the jail as dangerous; and
.B install \-\-refresh
puts back an agent's attachment that was taken out.
A command an agent's own sandbox already holds on macOS still runs in that
sandbox.
.PP
To set it where a user cannot edit it, an administrator writes the same keys
to the managed settings,
.I /Library/Application Support/Golem/managed\-shield.json
on macOS,
.I /etc/golem/managed\-shield.json
on Linux, or
.I C:\eProgram Files\eGolem\emanaged\-shield.json
on Windows.
Each key it names outranks
.IR ~/.golem/shield.json ;
.B lockdown
and
.B require_jail
only tighten.
On macOS and Linux the file and its directory must belong to root and be
writable by no one else, and the file must not be a link, or it is ignored.
.SH PROFILES
A profile decides
.BR cloud_credentials ,
.BR writes ,
.B network
and
.BR allowed_hosts ;
any of those the file names itself outranks it.
A file with no
.B profile
is read as written.
.TP
.B standard
Writes kept to the project, temp and the tool caches; cloud credentials and
the network open.
.TP
.B strict
As
.BR standard ,
without cloud credentials.
.TP
.B allowlist
As
.BR strict ,
with the network held to
.BR allowed_hosts ,
which it sets to
.BR @model\-apis ,
.B @package\-registries
and
.BR @git\-hosts .
A setting this version does not know, or a file that is not valid JSON, locks
every command down until it is fixed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.golem/bin/golem-exec
The installed shim, with the
.B golem\-zsh
and
.B golem\-bash
links (on Windows,
.IR bash.exe ).
.TP
.I ~/.golem/shield.json
The settings.
.TP
.IR "/Library/Application Support/Golem/managed\-shield.json" ", " /etc/golem/managed\-shield.json
The managed settings, which outrank the user's.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/settings.json
Where Claude Code is pointed at the shim.
.TP
.IR ~/.codex/hooks.json ", " ~/.gemini/settings.json ", " ~/.cursor/hooks.json ", " ~/.copilot/hooks/golem-shield.json
Where the hook is registered with Codex, Gemini CLI, Cursor and Copilot CLI.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GOLEM_WITNESS_URL
Where the Golem agent answers, on this machine only; by default
http://127.0.0.1:39393.
With an agent there, it decides each session's policy and
.I ~/.golem/shield.json
is not read.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when a command fails, 2 for a usage error.
Run as a shell, it exits with the command's status, or 126 when the jail could
not be built and the command was not run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR golem\-check (1)
.PP
